--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Divergences from docs/m1-boot.log (same source, little-endian arm64 host)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every line of narration is identical except the twelve differences below.  Nine
of them are the endianness dividend arriving; two are pointer width; one is the
disc's path.

1.  AUDIO -- the headline.  The host prints

        MSM(Sound Manager) Error:Error Code -121
        port> little-endian host: the game's own MSM parser cannot read the
              disc's big-endian sound bank; continuing without audio

    (MSM_ERR_INVALIDFILE: msmSysInit reads the version out of
    sound/mpgcsnd.msm as 0x02000000 instead of 2 and gives up on the first
    field.)  The G4 prints NEITHER LINE.  msmSysInit succeeds: it parses the
    sound bank header, loads the base group, and brings MusyX up.  The
    `host:`-prefixed patch in patches.txt that stops HuAudInit hanging is
    unreachable on this target, exactly as §9.6 predicted.

2.  Ten new stubs appear in the surface report, in the order MusyX asks for
    them -- AIInit, sndSetHooks, sndInit, AIRegisterDMACallback,
    sndStreamAllocEx (x2), sndAuxCallbackPrepareReverbHI,
    sndAuxCallbackPrepareDelay, sndSetAuxProcessingCallbacks, sndOutputMode
    (x2), sndVolume.  56 distinct stubs on the host, 66 on the G4; 111 calls
    against 123.  Every one of them is a real MusyX entry point the host build
    has never reached, so the G4 is the only place the audio surface can be
    measured at all.  Two of them had to be told to answer TRUE before the
    boot got past them -- see "the two fixes" below.

3.  REL HEADERS -- the parse the port will live or die by at M2.  The DLL
    module dump the failure path prints is the game's own read of
    dll/bootdll.rel, through `-malign-natural` structs, from a PowerPC FILE*
    on the NKit image.  Host against G4, and against the raw bytes at disc
    offset 0xbee90e4 read independently with Python:

        field                    host (LE)      G4 (BE)     raw disc
        ID                       0x01000000     0x00000001  1
        LinkPrev                 0x48000000     0x00000000  0
        LinkNext                 0x00000000     0x00000000  0
        Section num              788529152      14          14
        Section info tbl ofst    0x02000000     0x00000048  0x48
        nameOffset               0x5c000000     0x00000000  0
        nameSize                 -1654915072    47          47
        version                  0x4d7a0000     0x00000002  2
        bss Size                 0x00000101     0x0000005c  0x5c
        rel Offset               0x00000000     0x00005c9d  0x5c9d
        imp Offset               0x54000000     0x00007a4d  0x7a4d
        prolog Section           0              1           1
        epilog Section           0              1           1
        unresolved Section       0              0           0
        prolog func              0x08000000     0x00000000  0
        epilog func              0x00000000     0x00000054  0x54
        unresolved func          0x00000000     0x00000000  0

    Sixteen consecutive fields of two structures, every one of them right on
    the G4 and every one of them wrong on the host.  That is the alignment
    proof and the endianness proof in the same table: OSModuleInfo is eight
    u32s and OSModuleHeader adds four u32s, three u8s, a pad byte and three
    more u32s, and the compiler laid them out so that the game's own loader
    reads the file with no fixups at all.

4.  MEMORY -- pointer width, and it favours the G4.

        objman>Used Memory Size:00012200   host
        objman>Used Memory Size:000121E0   G4       (32 bytes less)

    Two live objects, sixteen bytes smaller each: the game's structures hold
    pointers, and on the 32-bit target they are the size the GameCube's are.
    The G4 number is the one that should match a real console.  The three heap
    sizes are identical (0x240000 / 0xa80000 / 0x580000), as is "Rest Memory
    580000" and "left memory space 2383KB(2441120)".

5.  Addresses.  The host maps MEM1 wherever mmap puts it (0x59.. this run);
    on the G4 it lands at 0x192c000 and the whole process lives below 32 bits,
    which is the arrangement HUPROCESS's u32 stack pointers assume natively --
    port_call_on_stack's 4 GB-window guard has nothing to do here.

6.  The disc path, which is just where the image sits on each machine.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The two fixes this first run needed
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The very first G4 run stopped 40 lines earlier than the host, at

        HuMem> Failed OSAlloc left space
        MSM(Sound Manager) Error:Error Code -31

and spun there.  Both were port bugs the host build cannot see, and both are
one-liners.

a.  OSCheckHeap's answer was not allocatable.  HuMemInitAll's last act is
    `OSAlloc(OSCheckHeap(h))` -- ask what is left, then ask for exactly that.
    OSAllocFromHeap rounds requests up to 32 bytes; the block header is 12
    bytes on the 32-bit target, so a free total is generically 20 (mod 32) and
    the round-up overshoots by 12.  On the 64-bit host the 16-byte header left
    the total already 32-aligned by luck, so heap 4 was created there and never
    on the G4.  port/src/os/os_arena.c now rounds OSCheckHeap's answer down to
    the allocation granularity.  Host output is unchanged (it was aligned
    already); the G4 now reports the same 2441120 and creates heap 4.

b.  A generated stub answered FALSE where MusyX answers TRUE.  Only reachable
    because (1) worked: msmSysSetAuxParam does
    `result = sndAuxCallbackPrepareReverbHI(...); if (result == FALSE) return TRUE;`
    and its caller turns that into MSM_ERR_INVALID_AUXPARAM (-31), which makes
    HuAudInit spin in `while (1)`.  gen_stubs.py already had a RETURN_OVERRIDES
    table for the typed stubs; the four sndAuxCallbackPrepare* entry points are
    added to it and the untyped generator now honours it too.  This is a
    placeholder answer, not an implementation: the aux effects do nothing until
    the MusyX SAL replacement lands.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What was proved on hardware, and what was not
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

PROVED

  * gcsetjmp / gclongjmp on Darwin PowerPC.  `Start New OVL 1` is printed by
    omMasterInit after HuPrcSleep hands control away and the scheduler hands it
    back, so the coroutine switch in port/src/os/jmp_ppc_darwin.s makes a full
    round trip on real hardware with the real ABI.  Everything after that line
    -- three heap dumps, objectsetup, the REL request -- runs on the fabricated
    HUPROCESS stack.
  * -malign-natural against disc data: the sixteen-field table above.
  * DVD from the NKit image on a PowerPC FILE*: the FST parses to the same 357
    files as on the host, the game resolves all of its boot data files without
    panicking, and it reads 31,325 bytes of dll/bootdll.rel from disc offset
    0xbee90e4 correctly.
  * The watchdog: fires at exactly the requested second (2.010 s wall for
    --watchdog 2, three runs) and prints a usable pc/sp with the
    image base for atos.
  * The crash handler links and installs; nothing crashed, so its backtrace
    path is still unexercised on this target.

NOT PROVED

  * ARAM does no work at boot.  `port> ARInit: 16 MB of ARAM` is the only ARAM
    line: nothing in the M1 boot issues an ARQ transfer, so the 16 MB block is
    allocated and indexed but never read or written.  The first real ARAM
    traffic is MusyX sample upload, which is M3.
  * dlclose on 10.5 (PLAN risk #2).  --reltest needs the REL loader, which was
    not on fork/ppc-port when this was captured.
  * Anything visual.  M1 has no window; the 66 GX entry points are counted, not
    drawn.

